[{"id": "R025", "text": "the instruments disagree. the wrench taps my thigh. i press the edge with the other. the explanation says the key was loose under the bench. the second says the old one. i press the old one harder. the key stays loose. the two explanations clash. i tap the bench with the old one, then the new one. the two sparks. i listen to the new one. the old one says the key is under the bench. the conclusion says the old one is the one that breaks."}, {"id": "R026", "text": "I turn the two pieces between finger and thumb. One edge catches, then drops. I hold the smaller one against the cloth and leave the other alone. The difference feels smaller than the argument."}, {"id": "R027", "text": "i keep the two explanations side by the edge and mark the instrument with ‘not clear.’ i fold the paper and write ‘not clear’ on the other side. i trust the edge case and keep the second one until the end."}, {"id": "R028", "text": "i tap the table edge twice with my fingertip, then hold the two pieces apart and write the difference beside the failure. “the second one,” i say, and tap the paper with my fingertip once. “i keep the first in my pocket.” i leave the explanation behind and write the instrument name beside the failure on the table edge."}, {"id": "R029", "text": "I stop and look at the two explanations. small amounts. one is the same, but the other is different. i put the smaller one in my pocket and pocket the larger one, but not yet. i test the smaller one with a rusted bolt, then leave it in the failure. the difference stays in the failure. the conclusion is still the same, but the small thing is waiting to be seen."}, {"id": "R030", "text": "i hold one instrument near the door and fold the paper with the other. the paper changes shape, so i fold the other one. the two explanations say the same thing, so i fold one into my palm and leave the other aside. the fold has become a story now. i keep folding until one of them fits my hand."}, {"id": "R031", "text": "I mark the mismatched contact with grease and leave it that way. I run one short test with one fresh stick, then leave the old explanation beside the bench. I keep the scratched edge beside the bench, leave the mismatched stick beside the bench, and watch where it catches."}, {"id": "R032", "text": "1. check the small amounts. 2. one watch: slow. 3. check the line. 4. check the square. 5. i keep it tidy. 6. ask the watch for the date. 7. one small mark. 8. i write it down. 9. test the small mark. 10. check the watch. slow. 11. keep it simple."}, {"id": "R033", "text": "i move the two pieces slowly and keep the one I leave behind. i write the difference on a loose sketchbook page and keep the other in my pocket. i wait for the other to stop moving."}, {"id": "R034", "text": "i hold the two explanations apart and trace the edge with my thumbnail. “the smaller one catches first,” i say, stopping when i find a faint scratch that feels like a tiny screw. “the other one keeps moving.” i leave the old one beside the instrument and watch the key stick move when i tap it once."}, {"id": "R035", "text": "i write the small amount on the test sheet, then check the explanation. i say sorry to the instrument, then leave it alone. i photograph the fault, then write the verdict on the test sheet. i keep the two explanations separate and leave the one that matters."}, {"id": "R036", "text": "the gap widens. i touch the wrench against my thigh, then stop. “one,” i say, though the other says no. i lean closer again, then stop again."}]
